On Freitag, 27. M=E4rz 2020 14:55:08 CET Mathieu Desnoyers wrote:
> Last time I checked, the main issue with perf's handling of cases that la=
ck
> frame pointers is that they grab a ~2-4kB worth of stack data into the
> trace, and then post-process it with libunwind. This might be OK for
> sampling use-cases, but is not something I consider appropriate for traci=
ng
> due to considerably increased use of tracing throughput.
>=20
> Has the situation changed on the perf side ?

Perf still does this. But heaptrack, of which I'm the main author, uses=20
libunwind to unwind on every heap allocation and it's plenty fast. Obviousl=
y=20
the impact is measureable and it's far away from even close to zero overhea=
d.=20
But it's super useful. I guess having that capability in LTTng would be=20
similarly useful and if it's documented to have an overhead people can use =
it=20
with care.

Cheers

